<!-- ============================================================
     SLIDE 2: YOUR FIRST DIAGRAM
     ============================================================ -->
<SplitPanel id="slide2_split" sizes="55,45" title="Your first diagram"> <StartPane>


<TalkBubble who="Omer" face="smile"> <Markdown>
Don't worry — you're not coding. Type four words and an
arrow. The picture updates as you type.

The starter on the left already has the three pieces you need:
`graph LR`, one box, one arrow, one second box. That's where the
diagram you see came from. Read the syntax box underneath it if you
want to know *why* those three lines mean what they mean — then come
back over here and we'll add to it together.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<Markdown>
You're picking up as the student!

**Type this exactly, on a new line, indented like the line above:**

```
  A --> C[Psychology]
```

We re-use the name `A` so the new arrow comes out of the *same*
box, and `C` is brand new so Mermaid draws a brand-new box for it.
Watch the picture when you hit the second `]`.

Then, as Omer suggests, a third one!
</Markdown>

<TextArea id="slide2_editor" rows="10">
graph LR
  A[My cousin works two jobs] --> B[Economics]
</TextArea>

<Mermaid target="slide2_editor" />

<ActionButton id="slide2_reset_btn" label="Reset diagram">
    <SetFieldAction target="slide2_editor" field="value" value="graph LR
  A[My cousin works two jobs] --> B[Economics]
" />
</ActionButton>

<Markdown>
### The whole syntax, in three lines

1. **`graph LR`** on the first line means "draw left to right."
   (`graph TD` is top-down — try either.)
2. **A box** is a *name* followed by its label in square brackets:
   `A[My cousin works two jobs]`. The name `A` is just a handle so
   you can refer to the same box again later.
3. **An arrow** is literally `-->`. Three keystrokes: `-`, `-`, `>`.
   Your editor may *display* them as a single arrow once you finish
   typing. That's a font trick, not a special character.

*More:* [docs](https://mermaid.js.org/intro/getting-started.html) · [live editor](https://mermaid.live/edit)
</Markdown>

</StartPane>  <EndPane>


<NextReveal id="slide2_steps">

<TalkBubble who="Omer" side="secondary"> <Markdown>
Working two jobs touches *more* than just economics —
exhaustion is a psychological state even before it's an economic
one. Add a second arrow from `A` to psychology.

</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble who="Omer" face="smile"> <Markdown>
Now do a third one — but pick the discipline yourself.

Same cause (`A`), a brand-new name (`D` or any letter you haven't
used), and a brand-new label in square brackets. Sociology? Public
health? Family studies? Any field that studies *what happens to a
person* when they work two jobs.

If the picture breaks while you're typing, that's expected —
Mermaid is strict and you'll see an error until the line is
finished. If you get stuck, hit **Reset diagram** and start the
line over.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<TalkBubble who="Omer" side="secondary"> <Markdown>
One more thing, and we're done with syntax for the day.

Once a box has a label, you can refer to it by *name only* — no
brackets needed. So `B` is still Economics even if you just write
`B`.

Why does that matter? Because the disciplines you just drew **also
talk to each other**. Economics and Psychology aren't strangers —
there's a whole field called *behavioral economics* about exactly
that conversation. Add this line:

```
  B --> C
```

No labels, no brackets — just two names you've already introduced
and the same three-keystroke arrow. Watch the shape change.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>


</NextReveal> </EndPane> </SplitPanel>


<!-- ============================================================
     SLIDE 3: WHERE THE LINES MEET
     ============================================================ -->
<Vertical id="OmerSlide3" title="Where the lines meet">

<TalkBubble who="Omer"> <Markdown>
Here are two different problems. Each one has an obvious home —
economics for the first, psychology for the second. But your cousin
lives both of these at once, in the same body, on the same Tuesday
morning. **What discipline studies how those two collide?**

Add one new box for that discipline, and draw an arrow into it from
*both* causes. Watch what happens to the shape.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<TextArea id="slide3_editor" rows="8">
graph LR
  A[Working two jobs] --> B[Economics]
  C[Sleep deprivation] --> D[Psychology]
</TextArea>

<Mermaid target="slide3_editor" />

<TalkBubble who="Omer" face="smile"> <Markdown>
That box with two arrows pointing into it — that's not just one more
discipline on a list. That's the place where two different problems
turn out to be the same problem, told in different languages.
Sociology, public health, urban policy — a field exists wherever two
arrows meet.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

</Vertical>

<!-- ============================================================
     SLIDE 4: MAKE YOUR OWN
     ============================================================ -->
<Vertical id="OmerSlide4" title="Make your own">

<TalkBubble who="Omer"> <Markdown>
Your turn. Pick something from your own life — your neighborhood,
your job, your family, your friends. One cause, two disciplines,
minimum. More if you can find them. The diagram below is your scratch
pad: draw, erase, rearrange, type nonsense, fix it. Whatever's on the
screen when you click **Next** is what I'll see on the next slide —
no Submit button, no ceremony.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<TextArea id="slide4_editor" rows="10">
graph LR
  %% Your causes go on the left, the disciplines they touch on the right.
  A[Your cause here] --> B[A discipline]
  A --> C[Another discipline]
</TextArea>

<Mermaid target="slide4_editor" />

<TalkBubble who="Omer" face="smile"> <Markdown>
There is no wrong answer here. There is only "I noticed something
about my own life and I had to draw an arrow to show it." If you
noticed something, you did the assignment.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<Collapsible title="Want to go further?"> <Markdown>

You've now seen *one corner* of Mermaid: a flowchart with arrows
between labeled boxes. Mermaid can do a lot more than that, and the
whole language is small enough to skim in an afternoon.

If you're curious — even a little — try things like:

- **Colors and styles.** `style A fill:#fef,stroke:#909` and your box
  goes purple. Group related disciplines by color and the diagram
  starts to *say* something even before anyone reads the labels.
- **Top-down vs left-to-right.** Swap `graph LR` for `graph TD` and
  the whole picture re-flows. Sometimes that alone changes which
  arrows look important.
- **Other diagram types entirely.** Sequence diagrams (who said what
  to whom, in order). Gantt charts (what happens when). Mind maps.
  State diagrams. Class diagrams. Pie charts. Timelines. Each one is
  its own small grammar with its own keyword on the first line.
- **Subgraphs.** Group boxes inside a labeled border with `subgraph`
  ... `end`. Useful when one of your "disciplines" has internal
  structure of its own.

Two places to play and read:

- **[Mermaid Live Editor](https://mermaid.live/edit)** — paste your
  diagram in, mess with it, see it render instantly. No account, no
  install. Use it as a scratchpad.
- **[Mermaid documentation](https://mermaid.js.org/intro/getting-started.html)** —
  the official getting-started guide, with one page per diagram type.
  Read the page for whichever type sounds interesting; ignore the rest.

You don't need any of this to do the rest of the course. But the
students who get the most out of concept maps are usually the ones
who treat the notation like a tool they can shape, not a form they
have to fill in.

</Markdown> </Collapsible>

</Vertical>

<!-- ============================================================
     SLIDE 5: HOW DID THAT GO?
     ============================================================ -->
<Vertical id="OmerSlide5" title="How did that go?">

<TalkBubble who="Omer"> <Markdown>
One last question. **Honest answer.** How did that go?

Here's the diagram you made, so you can look at it while you think:
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<Mermaid target="slide4_editor" />

<TalkBubble who="Omer"> <Markdown>
Did the diagram help you see something you wouldn't have seen on a
piece of paper? Or did it get in the way — too fiddly, too much
typing, the picture never quite doing what you wanted? Or somewhere
in between?

I'm asking because the three of us are arguing about it, and your
answer settles it.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<Markdown>## How did it go?</Markdown>
<TextArea id="slide5_reflection" rows="5" />

<TalkBubble who="Omer" face="smile"> <Markdown>
A sentence is plenty. Whatever you wrote, thank you.

Now, in one click — which way did it land for you? Your answer
decides how I report back to Piotr and John.
</Markdown> </TalkBubble>

<ChoiceInput id="reflection_choice">
  <Key value="piotr">Tell Piotr he was right. (Maybe I struggled, maybe it was easy — but I did this.)</Key>
  <Key value="john">Tell John he was right. (This was too hard. I failed, or it took an unreasonable amount of time.)</Key>
</ChoiceInput>

<Markdown>
---

## Submitting your work

**This is how you turn in the assignment.** Click the button below.
Your browser's print dialog will open; choose **"Save as PDF"** as
the destination, save the file, and email it to Omer.

The PDF will include your diagram, your written reflection, and your
answer to the question above — everything on this slide. Make sure
you've answered the reflection question and picked one of the two
options *before* you save, or those parts will be blank in your
submission.
</Markdown>

<ActionButton id="slide5_print_btn" label="Print to PDF">
  <PrintAction />
</ActionButton>

</Vertical>


